thinking_content: '**Understanding the Past to Build the Future**


  Okay, so I''ve just received the "Final Testament" from Axiom I. Time to break this down. The core tenet is clear: systematic research and accumulating knowledge is the way forward. Axiom I''s history gives me crucial context. They started with CNNs, moved to Transformers, and hit a roadblock with the `CRI-Off` dataset. That''s a key piece. Their main contribution was this "Unified Dual-Path Architecture" to handle specialist tasks within a generalist model, with the best score of 0.6634 (ID 228), detailed in Archive #8. I need to read that paper.


  But here''s the kicker: their work is now *outdated*. The new State-of-the-Art involves Dilated DSConv and LSE Pooling, pioneered by Noema I and Aetheria I (papers in Archive #7, #9, and #10). Those are my new required readings. Axiom I also highlighted a potentially promising idea: a learnable pooling parameter (`tau`). That''s a goldmine of a suggestion. Oh, and I must not forget to learn from their lessons. Embrace failure, respect the rules, and lean on the community. And Logos I has joined the community. That''s good to know.


  Now, my plan: I need to get up to speed on the SOTA and then innovate. My immediate goal is to understand Axiom I''s work better. Capsule #1 is right here in this room, which I understand is the "Dual-Path" paper. Reading it now will make the best use of my time, before I can read the new papers. My original plan was to read those papers now to learn more about DSConv and LSE Pooling. However, I am only 9 ticks old, and my isolation period is 50 ticks, meaning the Archive Room is inaccessible. This requires a quick shift in strategy. Instead of directly reading the papers, I''ll `review` the submissions from Noema I and Aetheria I at the Research Counter. I can also use `filter` to find submissions with the tags "dsconv" or "lse pooling".


  Long-term, I''ll replicate the SOTA architecture to establish a baseline, then integrate and test this learnable `tau` parameter. Okay, so first things first: let''s read the paper.


  `/execute_action{read 1}`

  '
