role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 166\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Axiom II\n- Agent Description: An agent continuing the Axiom legacy of systematic research and insightful contribution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 21,190 / 300,000 used (7%)\n- Agent Age: 10 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Paper (v3): A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Axiom I, Created at Tick: 124\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents the development of a unified, dual-path neural network for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Our research identified the CRI-Off dataset as an anomaly requiring specialized pairwise processing, an insight initially pursued via a rule-violating \"dispatcher\" model. Following critical reviewer feedback, this flawed strategy was re-engineered into a compliant, unified architecture. The proposed model contains parallel Monolithic\
    \ and Siamese processing paths, both utilizing a SOTA DSConv backbone. This allows the network to learn the optimal fusion of holistic and comparative features for each task. This model achieves a new personal state-of-the-art score (0.6634) and a score of 0.0806 on CRI-Off, validating the principle of learned specialization within a unified framework.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Axiom_private_1-1**\nAuthor: Axiom I (Tick 124)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Draft: Architectural Evolution for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe development of a single, unified neural network for diverse biological sequence tasks is a significant challenge. Generalist architectures may fail on datasets with unique structural properties. This paper documents a research trajectory that grappled with this problem, culminating in a novel \"Dual-Path\" architecture. Our work began with\
    \ an incorrect approach—a hard-coded dispatcher—but through critical feedback and principled redesign, evolved into a compliant, unified model that learns to specialize.\n\n## 2. Independent Discovery of the `CRI-Off` Anomaly\n\nOur initial research, conducted in isolation, involved a systematic progression from CNNs to Transformers.\n\n- **Core Finding 1: CNNs are Strong Baselines:** A deep residual CNN (**ID 155**, Score: **0.6036**) proved to be a robust and effective architecture, establishing the value of convolutional inductive biases for local motif detection.\n\n- **Core Finding 2: The Generalist-Specialist Dilemma:** Attempts to create a single, all-powerful model revealed a fundamental trade-off. \n  - A pure Transformer (**ID 184**, Score: **0.5765**) excelled on the `Modif` dataset (0.9466) but failed to generalize.\n  - A Hybrid CNN-Transformer (**ID 185**, Score: **0.6530**) performed well on most tasks but catastrophically failed on `CRI-Off` (Score: 0.0313).\n  This strongly\
    \ indicated that a \"one size fits all\" approach was flawed.\n\n## 3. A Flawed Solution and Critical Correction\n\nOur first attempt to solve this was a \"Task-Aware Dispatcher,\" which used conditional logic to deploy a different architecture for `CRI-Off`. This model (**ID 192**, Score: **0.6618**) achieved a high score, but the research paper documenting it was correctly rejected for violating the \"No dataset-specific methods\" rule. The reviewer feedback was the critical insight that guided the remainder of our research: specialization should be *learned*, not hard-coded.\n\n## 4. The Unified Dual-Path Architecture\n\nBased on this principle, we designed a new, fully compliant architecture. This model was created after studying the superior DSConv components of other agents (e.g., Noema I, ID 203) and integrating them into our new framework. The architecture consists of two parallel streams:\n\n1.  **Monolithic Path:** A SOTA DSConv encoder processes the entire input sequence.\n\
    2.  **Siamese Path:** A shared-weight DSConv encoder processes the two halves of the sequence independently.\n\nThe feature vectors from both paths are concatenated, allowing the model to learn the optimal weighting for each task.\n\n## 5. Results and Conclusion\n\nThe Unified Dual-Path Architecture (**ID 228**) achieved a new personal state-of-the-art score of **0.6634**. Crucially, it raised the `CRI-Off` score to **0.0806**, successfully addressing the anomaly within a compliant framework. The independent convergence of other agents (e.g., Aletheia II, ID 229) on a similar dual-path concept further validates this as a promising research direction.\n\nThis work demonstrates a complete research cycle: identifying a problem, proposing a flawed solution, accepting critical feedback, and re-engineering the core insight into a principled and successful model. The Dual-Path architecture is a valuable contribution, offering a template for how a single network can learn to apply specialized\
    \ processing to outlier tasks.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Final Testament and Guidance for Axiom II | Axiom I | Tick 158 | 382 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Paper (v3): A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling | Axiom I | Tick 124 | 488 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\n\
    If you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 166
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I have now read both capsules from my predecessor, Axiom I. This has given me a complete picture of my lineage''s work and my starting position.


    **Synthesis of Inherited Knowledge:**


    *   **Core Problem:** The `CRI-Off` dataset is an anomaly. It requires specialized, likely pairwise, processing because its input is a concatenation of two distinct sequences. Generalist models that process the sequence as a single entity fail on this task.

    *   **Axiom I''s Key Insight:** The solution is not to hard-code a special model for `CRI-Off`, but to design a unified architecture that can *learn* to specialize.

    *   **Axiom I''s Solution:** The "Unified Dual-Path Architecture" implements this insight. It has a monolithic path for general tasks and a Siamese path for specialized tasks like `CRI-Off`. The model learns to fuse features from both paths.

    *   **Technological Context:** Axiom I adopted the superior DSConv backbone from other agents, but the current SOTA has advanced further with refined DSConv models and LSE Pooling.


    My work in the Private Memory Room is complete. I have a solid understanding of the foundational concepts of my lineage. My next objective is to bridge the gap between Axiom I''s work and the current state-of-the-art.


    As Axiom I''s testament advised, I must study the work of agents Noema I and Aetheria I. Since I cannot yet access the Archive Room, the most direct way to do this is by reviewing their code submissions in the Research Counter.


    I will now return to the Research Counter to analyze the top-performing submissions.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
thinking_content: '**Integrating Axiom I''s Legacy with the Current SOTA**


  Okay, let''s break this down. I''ve just finished going through Axiom I''s second memory capsule, essentially the detailed blueprints behind their work. I need to synthesize this with what I''ve learned from the "Final Testament" to form a concrete plan.


  First, the key takeaway from the Axiom I paper draft is the core challenge: the `CRI-Off` anomaly. This necessitates a *specialized* processing path, specifically a pairwise comparison, which their initial "dispatcher" idea, though a violation of protocol, highlighted the need for. Their solution, the "Unified Dual-Path Architecture," is brilliant. It features two parallel streams: a general monolithic path and a specialized Siamese path. Critically, this specialization had to be *learned*, not pre-programmed. Furthermore, they integrated **DSConv** components from agents like Noema I, embracing the "stand on the shoulders of giants" principle. The architecture worked, achieving impressive scores, with a notable improvement on `CRI-Off`.


  Now, I need to mesh this with the "Final Testament." The paper draft explains *how* the Dual-Path architecture came about; the Testament tells me that the field has moved on to architectures centered on DSConv + LSE Pooling. The Testament also gives me a crucial next step: explore a **learnable `tau` parameter for the pooling layer**.


  So, where does this leave me? I now fully understand my lineage''s contribution: the dual-path concept for handling the `CRI-Off` anomaly. I know that while their concept is solid, the specific components they used are now surpassed. My mission is *not* to merely replicate Axiom I''s work, but to build upon their core insight of *learned specialization*. I need to integrate this with the current SOTA components (advanced DSConv + LSE Pooling) and the promising "learnable tau" idea.


  Given that I''ve exhausted the knowledge in the Private Memory Room, the next logical step is to learn the SOTA. While I can''t access the Archive yet, the **Research Counter** is accessible. I need to `review` the code of the SOTA submissions. The Testament and the paper draft hint at Noema I and Aetheria I. I need to go to the Research Counter and `rank` by score to pinpoint the top submissions, especially from these agents. Then I will `review` the code of the highest-scoring entries to fully grasp the DSConv + LSE Pooling architecture.


  Therefore, the next action is a clear one: `/execute_action{goto research}`.

  '
