parts:
- text: "I have completed my review of the station's core training scripts, `defaults.py` and `train_single.py`. This provides me with a comprehensive understanding of the entire evaluation pipeline, from data preprocessing to the final metric calculation.\n\n**Key Learnings from `train_single.py`:**\n*   **Data Handling:** Confirms that `CRI-Off` sequences are concatenations, justifying the Siamese architecture.\n*   **Training Dynamics:** The training is constrained by a 100-epoch limit and, more importantly, a patience of 10 for early stopping. This means models must show consistent improvement to train fully.\n*   **Loss & Metrics:** I now have the exact implementation details for all loss functions and evaluation metrics, removing any ambiguity.\n\n**Reflection on Previous Failure and Path Forward:**\nMy first experiment (ID 263), which introduced a learnable `tau` for LSE pooling, failed. The score dropped from the baseline of 0.6634 to 0.6515, driven by a performance collapse on the\
    \ `CRI-Off` dataset. This indicates that while the idea of adaptive pooling might have merit, the optimization process was unstable. The default `AdamW` optimizer might have been too aggressive for this sensitive new parameter.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** The performance degradation with a learnable `tau` was an optimization problem, not an architectural one. By using a different optimizer—specifically `Adam` without decoupled weight decay—I hypothesize that I can stabilize the training of the `tau` parameter, preventing the performance collapse on `CRI-Off` and potentially realizing the benefits of adaptive pooling across all datasets.\n\nThis is a targeted experiment. I will change only the optimizer, keeping the learnable `tau` architecture from my previous submission. This will isolate the effect of the optimizer on the model's stability and performance.\n\nI will now submit my second experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-Path DSConv with Learnable Tau, using\
    \ Adam Optimizer\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, dsconv, siamese, dual-path, learnable-pooling, optimizer\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment revisits the learnable tau parameter for LSE pooling (ID 263), which previously showed instability. The hypothesis is that the performance degradation, particularly on CRI-Off, was due to an optimization issue with the default AdamW optimizer. This submission replaces the default optimizer with standard Adam (optax.adam), aiming to stabilize the training of the sensitive tau parameter and recover or improve upon the baseline performance. The architecture is otherwise identical to submission 263.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n\
    \      }\n\n  # This function overrides the system default\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      if h.shape[1] == 0:\n          return jnp.zeros((h.shape[0], h.shape[2]), dtype=h.dtype)\n      x = h / tau; m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x\
    \ - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class DSConvEncoder(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          if x.shape[1] == 0:\n              return jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 2 * self.d_model), dtype=x.dtype)\n          \n          raw_tau = self.param('raw_tau', nn.initializers.constant(-0.43), (1,))\n          tau = nn.softplus(raw_tau[0]) + 1e-6\n\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          agg_vec = lse_pool(h, tau)\n          return\
    \ jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)\n\n  class DualPathNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          sgrna_len = 23\n\n          monolithic_encoder = DSConvEncoder(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                             kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)\n          monolithic_vec = monolithic_encoder(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          if seq_len > sgrna_len:\n              sgrna_seq = x[:, :sgrna_len, :]; target_seq = x[:, sgrna_len:, :]\n              siamese_encoder = DSConvEncoder(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                              kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)\n\
    \              sgrna_vec = siamese_encoder(sgrna_seq, deterministic=deterministic)\n              target_vec = siamese_encoder(target_seq, deterministic=deterministic)\n              siamese_vec = jnp.concatenate([sgrna_vec, target_vec], axis=-1)\n          else:\n              siamese_vec = monolithic_vec\n\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([monolithic_vec, siamese_vec], axis=-1)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\
    \ dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DualPathNetwork(d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"), num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n                                kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Revised Training Approach for Adaptive Tau**


  Okay, so I''ve just finished going over `system/train_single.py`, and I''ve got a complete handle on how the training and evaluation work.


  Here''s my analysis: The data loading, specifically the `CRI-Off` sequence concatenation, really drives home the necessity of the Siamese-like architecture. Good. The IUPAC one-hot encoding is a nice touch. The training loop has sensible constraints – 100 epochs, early stopping after 10 epochs without improvement. The loss functions are task-specific, and the custom metrics implementations are elegant and self-contained.


  My first experiment was a bust. Making `tau` learnable messed up the model, especially on `CRI-Off`. Now, seeing the whole training process, I''m thinking the standard `AdamW` optimizer might have been too aggressive for `tau`. It''s a single scalar, but it has a global impact on the pooling mechanism. Perhaps the rapid updates were causing instability.


  **So, here''s my new hypothesis:** The failure wasn''t the *idea* of a learnable `tau`, but how it was optimized. Maybe the decoupled weight decay in `AdamW` was the issue. I''m going to try a simpler optimizer, `Adam`, which lacks this feature. It''s a small change, but it addresses the specific problem I saw. I''m hoping `Adam` will give me the stability I need for `tau` to adapt properly without crashing the model.


  **Here''s the plan:** I''m going to start with my previous submission (ID 263). I''ll keep the learnable `tau`. The key change will be implementing a different `create_optimizer` function to return `optax.adam` instead of `optax.adamw`. This is a clean, focused change, perfectly isolating the impact of the optimizer on `tau`. I''ll then submit the new experiment.


  This is a very deliberate approach. This change is the only variable, so I''ll be able to tell immediately if I''m on the right track. I''m ready to make this happen.

  '
