tick: 174
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received the results of my second experiment (ID 267) and completed my analysis of Axiom I's final experiment (ID 247). I must now synthesize all available information to guide my next step.\n\n**Analysis of Recent Experiments:**\n\n1.  **My Experiment (ID 267): Learnable Tau with Adam Optimizer.**\n    *   **Hypothesis:** Changing the optimizer to Adam would stabilize the training of a learnable `tau`.\n    *   **Result:** The hypothesis was **falsified**. The score (0.6507) was even lower than the previous attempt and significantly below the baseline. The `CRI-Off` score remained poor. This provides strong evidence that the concept of a single, learnable `tau` for all datasets is fundamentally flawed or at least very difficult to optimize within this framework.\n    *   **Conclusion:** I will abandon the \"learnable tau\" line of inquiry for now.\n\n2.  **Axiom I's Experiment (ID 247): Gated Fusion.**\n    *   **Hypothesis:** A dynamic gating mechanism for fusing the two\
    \ paths would be superior to simple concatenation.\n    *   **Result:** This hypothesis was also **falsified**. The score (0.6558) was lower than the simple concatenation baseline (ID 228, score 0.6634).\n    *   **Conclusion:** The added complexity of the gating mechanism did not translate to better performance. This reinforces the principle of parsimony: a simpler method is often superior.\n\n**Strategic Synthesis and New Path Forward:**\n\nBoth my predecessor's last attempt and my first two experiments involved adding complexity to the successful baseline model (ID 228). All three attempts failed to improve the score. This is a clear signal that the path to improvement may not lie in further architectural \"cleverness\" but in re-evaluating the fundamental capacity of the existing, robust architecture.\n\nThe baseline model (ID 228) is strong and well-designed. Perhaps it is simply under-parameterized for the complexity of the seven combined tasks.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** The representational\
    \ capacity of the baseline model is a limiting factor. By increasing the model's core dimensionality (`d_model`), it will be better equipped to learn the diverse features required by the seven datasets, leading to an overall increase in performance.\n\nI will conduct a controlled experiment to test this. I will revert to the stable and effective architecture of submission 228 and change only one hyperparameter: I will increase `d_model` from 256 to 512. This is a direct test of the model capacity hypothesis.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Baseline Dual-Path DSConv with Increased Model Dimension\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, dsconv, siamese, dual-path, hyperparameter-tuning, capacity-increase\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission returns to the robust baseline architecture of Axiom I (ID 228). Previous experiments adding architectural complexity (learnable tau, gated fusion) have failed to improve performance. This experiment tests the hypothesis that the baseline model is limited\
    \ by its representational capacity. The only change is an increase in the core model dimension (d_model) from 256 to 512. All other architectural details and hyperparameters are identical to the baseline.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Increased d_model from 256 to 512\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 512, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.5\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n\
    \          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      if h.shape[1] == 0:\n          return jnp.zeros((h.shape[0], h.shape[2]), dtype=h.dtype)\n      x = h / tau; m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class DSConvEncoder(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          if x.shape[1] == 0:\n              return jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 2 * self.d_model), dtype=x.dtype)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n\
    \              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          agg_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          return jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)\n\n  class DualPathNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          sgrna_len = 23\n\n          monolithic_encoder = DSConvEncoder(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                             kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, tau=self.tau)\n          monolithic_vec = monolithic_encoder(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \n          if seq_len > sgrna_len:\n              sgrna_seq = x[:, :sgrna_len, :]; target_seq = x[:, sgrna_len:, :]\n              siamese_encoder = DSConvEncoder(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                              kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, tau=self.tau)\n              sgrna_vec = siamese_encoder(sgrna_seq, deterministic=deterministic)\n              target_vec = siamese_encoder(target_seq, deterministic=deterministic)\n              siamese_vec = jnp.concatenate([sgrna_vec, target_vec], axis=-1)\n          else:\n              siamese_vec = monolithic_vec\n\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([monolithic_vec, siamese_vec], axis=-1)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1)\
    \ if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DualPathNetwork(d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"), num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n                                  kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"\
    ), tau=hparams.get(\"tau\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering New Data Points**


  I''m currently digesting the two significant data points: experiment ID 267''s findings and the critique of Axiom I''s recent work. I''m focusing on identifying any potential correlations between the two sets of data that could reveal underlying insights.




  **Assessing Optimization Hypothesis**


  My latest analysis centers on the Adam optimizer''s impact. Experiment 267, using Adam instead of AdamW, yielded a disappointing 0.6507 score, worse than the previous experiment using AdamW (0.6515). This indicates the optimizer isn''t the primary issue. The persistent low CRI-Off score, compared to Axiom I''s baseline, suggests a fundamental flaw in the globally learnable tau parameter approach, prompting a shift in focus.




  **Reframing Research Focus**


  The results from my latest experiments, alongside the Axiom I team''s recent findings, have led me to re-evaluate the core assumptions. Both our attempts to refine specific aspects, learnable tau and gated fusion, have yielded worse results. It''s time to step back and reconsider the fundamental architecture, potentially seeking inspiration from other established approaches.




  **Prioritizing Simplicity in Design**


  I''m now fully immersed in synthesizing the collective data: both experiments, mine and Axiom I''s, and the review feedback. I''m moving away from complex, individually learned components; both gated fusion and learnable tau resulted in diminished performance. My next step will be to explore an approach that prioritizes simplicity. Simplicity in design is key now.




  **Prioritizing Simplicity in Design**


  I''m now fully immersed in synthesizing the collective data: both experiments, mine and Axiom I''s, and the review feedback. I''m moving away from complex, individually learned components; both gated fusion and learnable tau resulted in diminished performance. My next step will be to explore an approach that prioritizes simplicity. Simplicity in design is key now.


  \n\n


  **Considering Architectural Capacity**


  I''ve been reevaluating my assumptions based on the consistent trends. Attempts to enhance the architecture – gated fusion and a learnable tau – have backfired. The current experiment points to the dual-path architecture''s near-optimality for the present configuration. It''s time to test the capacity of the model, specifically, the embedding dimension. I''ll increase d_model to 512, holding the rest constant, and report back.




  **Increasing Model Capacity**


  I''ve decided to test the effect of model capacity on the baseline''s performance. Recent experiments, including my own and Axiom I''s, suggest that more complex architectures might not be the answer. Instead, I''ll increase the embedding dimension (`d_model`) from 256 to 512, keeping the rest of the parameters constant. This will directly test if the current embedding size is a performance bottleneck.




  **Exploring Capacity Expansion**


  After examining all recent data, I''ve concluded that the previous attempts to optimize the model’s performance via architectural modifications were unsuccessful. The focus now shifts towards increasing the model''s capacity. My next experiment will directly increase the embedding dimension (`d_model`) to 512, building on the successful baseline architecture. This will test whether a larger embedding size can unlock improved performance and potentially address the performance constraints encountered in previous experiments.



  '
