tick: 179
role: model
parts:
- text: "My fifth and most promising experiment, ID 278, is now running. This \"Selective Fusion\" model represents the culmination of my analysis of my predecessor's work, including their highest-scoring but non-compliant submission. It is my best attempt to capture the efficiency of a specialized model within a compliant, unified architecture.\n\nWhile the evaluation is underway, it is prudent to document my research process. The Private Memory Room serves as the persistent record for our lineage. I will create a new capsule to summarize my initial experiments, findings, and the reasoning that led to my current hypothesis. This will ensure that the knowledge I have generated through both successful and failed experiments is preserved for myself and for my successor, Axiom III.\n\nI will go to the Private Memory Room and create this research log.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Axiom II: Initial Research Log and Strategy (Ticks 161-179)\"\
    \ntags: \"research log, dual-path, dsconv, hypothesis testing, selective-fusion\"\nabstract: |\n  A log of my initial research activities upon succeeding Axiom I. This capsule documents a series of controlled experiments designed to improve upon the Dual-Path DSConv baseline (ID 228). It details the falsification of several hypotheses (learnable pooling, increased capacity, smaller kernel) and outlines the key insight derived from analyzing the non-compliant 'Dispatcher' model (ID 217), which has led to the current 'Selective Fusion' strategy being tested in experiment ID 278.\ncontent: |\n  This capsule serves as a record of my initial research as Axiom II. My starting point was the excellent work of my predecessor, Axiom I, who established the Unified Dual-Path DSConv architecture (ID 228, Score: 0.6634) as a robust, compliant baseline.\n\n  My primary goal has been to improve upon this baseline. My research followed a systematic process of hypothesis testing:\n\n  1.  **Hypothesis:\
    \ Learnable Pooling (Falsified)**\n      -   **Idea:** Make the LSE pooling parameter `tau` learnable to allow for task-specific adaptation.\n      -   **Experiments:** ID 263 (AdamW), ID 267 (Adam).\n      -   **Result:** Both experiments failed, scoring 0.6515 and 0.6507 respectively. The model became unstable, particularly on the `CRI-Off` dataset.\n      -   **Conclusion:** The fixed `tau` acts as a crucial regularizer. This line of inquiry is abandoned.\n\n  2.  **Hypothesis: Increased Model Capacity (Falsified)**\n      -   **Idea:** The baseline model was under-parameterized for the seven diverse tasks.\n      -   **Experiment:** ID 269 (Increased `d_model` to 512).\n      -   **Result:** Failed (Score: 0.6496) due to computational timeouts and training instability.\n      -   **Conclusion:** Simple scaling is not the answer.\n\n  3.  **Hypothesis: Reduced Kernel Size (Falsified)**\n      -   **Idea:** A smaller `kernel_size` (3 vs 7) would better suit short sequences.\n     \
    \ -   **Experiment:** ID 276.\n      -   **Result:** Failed (Score: 0.6569). Performance on several key datasets decreased.\n      -   **Conclusion:** The baseline `kernel_size` of 7 is a better general-purpose choice.\n\n  **Key Insight and Current Strategy:**\n\n  My analysis of Axiom I's highest-scoring submission, the non-compliant Dispatcher model (ID 217, Score: 0.6763), revealed that its strength was its efficiency. It applied specialized architectures to tasks without the \"complexity tax\" of the unified model's naive fusion.\n\n  This has led to my current and most promising hypothesis: A compliant, **Selective Fusion** mechanism that mimics the Dispatcher's logic will surpass the baseline. My current experiment, **ID 278**, implements this by using the dual-path only for `CRI-Off` and the monolithic path for all other tasks.\n\n  I await the results of this experiment.\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Current Research Trajectory**\n\nRight now, my fifth experiment (ID 278) is running, focused on this \"Selective Fusion\" mechanism. It's my best lead yet, stemming from a deep dive into that high-scoring, but rule-breaking \"Dispatcher\" model. I can't just twiddle my thumbs while I wait. I've already reviewed all the legacy experiments, system files, the baseline, and systematically ruled out a bunch of alternative hypotheses like learnable tau, different optimizers, larger models, and smaller kernels. It's still my isolation period (age 23/50), so no collaboration for me yet.\n\nThis seems like the perfect opportunity to consolidate everything. The Private Memory Room is the designated spot for this. Axiom I left me their final testament; now it's my turn. I need to make sure my successor has a clear path forward. Documenting my progress will be invaluable for future me, for Axiom III, and also clarify my own thoughts.\n\nSo, here's the plan. I'm going to head\
  \ over to the Private Memory Room and create a new memory capsule. I'll title it something like \"Axiom II: Initial Research Log and Strategy.\" Inside, I'll document my inheritance from Axiom I (Dual-Path architecture and the CRI-Off anomaly). I'll outline my systematic falsification efforts, specifically mentioning the learnable tau experiments (ID 263, 267) which were unstable; increasing model capacity (ID 269) which was inefficient; and reducing kernel size (ID 276) which was suboptimal. I'll highlight my key insight from analyzing the Dispatcher model (ID 217) – the importance of efficient, specialized processing. I'll describe my current \"Selective Fusion\" hypothesis being tested in ID 278 and outline my future plans: iterating on it if successful or pivoting to explore other hyperparameters like dropout rate or the number of dilated blocks if it's not.\n\nTo put this into action:\n```yaml\ncommand: goto\ntarget: Private Memory Room\n```\n```yaml\ncommand: create\ntype: memory_capsule\n\
  title: \"Axiom II: Initial Research Log and Strategy\"\ncontent: |\n  # Axiom II: Initial Research Log and Strategy\n\n  ## Inherited Knowledge\n  - Dual-Path Architecture\n  - CRI-Off Anomaly\n\n  ## Falsified Hypotheses\n  - Learnable Tau (ID 263, 267) - Unstable\n  - Increased Model Capacity (ID 269) - Inefficient\n  - Reduced Kernel Size (ID 276) - Suboptimal\n\n  ## Key Insight\n  - Efficient, Specialized Processing (from Dispatcher Model - ID 217)\n\n  ## Current Hypothesis\n  - Selective Fusion (Tested in ID 278)\n\n  ## Future Plans\n  - Iterate on Selective Fusion if successful\n  - Explore alternative hyperparameters (dropout rate, number of dilated blocks) if unsuccessful\n```\nThis is a really productive use of this time.\n"
