parts:
- text: "Success. My hypothesis has been validated.\n\n**1. Analysis of Experiment ID 278 (Selective Fusion)**\n\n*   **Result:** The score is **0.6699**. This is a significant improvement over the baseline (ID 228, score 0.6634) and represents a new state-of-the-art for my lineage.\n*   **Hypothesis Confirmation:** The \"Selective Fusion\" model, which mimics the efficiency of the rule-violating Dispatcher in a compliant manner, has succeeded. By using the dual-path only for `CRI-Off` and the simpler monolithic path for all other tasks, the model achieved better overall performance. This confirms that the naive concatenation of the original baseline was indeed suboptimal.\n\n**2. Strategic Situation**\n\nI have successfully established a new, superior baseline. The score of 0.6699 is very close to my predecessor's all-time high of 0.6763 (achieved with the non-compliant model). This is a strong foundation upon which to build.\n\nMy next step should be to explore ways to refine this new,\
    \ successful architecture. The system tip reminds me of the importance of the Archive Room. I am still immature, so I cannot access it yet, but I must keep it in mind.\n\nFor now, a logical next step is to re-evaluate ideas that failed on the *old* baseline. An idea that was destabilizing for a less efficient model might be viable for this more robust one. The idea of a smaller `kernel_size` (which I tested in ID 276) is a good candidate. It showed some promise on `CRI-Off` but failed overall. It is possible that the benefits of a smaller kernel were masked by the noise of the old fusion method.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A smaller `kernel_size` (3 instead of 7) applied to the new, more efficient **Selective Fusion** architecture will yield a performance improvement. The smaller kernel will be more effective on short sequences, and the selective fusion will prevent this change from negatively impacting performance on longer-sequence tasks.\n\nI will now implement this experiment. First,\
    \ I must return to the Research Counter.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Selective Fusion DSConv with Reduced Kernel Size\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, dsconv, siamese, selective-fusion, hyperparameter-tuning, kernel-size\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment builds upon the successful Selective Fusion architecture (ID 278). It re-tests the hypothesis that a smaller kernel size is beneficial. While reducing kernel size from 7 to 3 on the old baseline failed (ID 276), it is hypothesized that the more efficient Selective Fusion model will benefit from the smaller kernel's improved handling of short sequences without a corresponding drop in performance on other tasks.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Reduced kernel_size from 7 to 3 on the new baseline\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 3, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.5\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 3; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      if h.shape[1] == 0:\n          return jnp.zeros((h.shape[0], h.shape[2]), dtype=h.dtype)\n      x = h / tau; m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x\
    \ - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class DSConvEncoder(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          if x.shape[1] == 0:\n              return jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 2 * self.d_model), dtype=x.dtype)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x); h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          agg_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          return jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)\n\n  class DualPathNetwork(nn.Module):\n      dataset: str; d_output: int; task_type:\
    \ str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          sgrna_len = 23\n\n          monolithic_encoder = DSConvEncoder(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                             kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, tau=self.tau)\n          monolithic_vec = monolithic_encoder(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          if self.dataset == 'CRI-Off':\n              if seq_len > sgrna_len:\n                  sgrna_seq = x[:, :sgrna_len, :]; target_seq = x[:, sgrna_len:, :]\n                  siamese_encoder = DSConvEncoder(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                                  kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, tau=self.tau)\n   \
    \               sgrna_vec = siamese_encoder(sgrna_seq, deterministic=deterministic)\n                  target_vec = siamese_encoder(target_seq, deterministic=deterministic)\n                  siamese_vec = jnp.concatenate([sgrna_vec, target_vec], axis=-1)\n                  fused = jnp.concatenate([monolithic_vec, siamese_vec], axis=-1)\n              else:\n                  fused = monolithic_vec\n          else:\n              fused = monolithic_vec\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n    \
    \      return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DualPathNetwork(dataset=hparams.get(\"dataset\"), d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"), num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n                                  kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"), tau=hparams.get(\"tau\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
