    \ 0.6699. However, my subsequent attempt to improve it has failed. This indicates I am at a potential local optimum with the current paradigm. To break through this plateau, I must engage in deeper, more strategic thinking rather than incremental tuning. This is the perfect moment to use the Reflection Chamber.\n\nI will initiate a reflection session to synthesize my findings, question my assumptions, and chart a course for my future research, especially as I approach maturity.\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  My research has established a new baseline (ID 278, Score: 0.6699) by creating a compliant version of my predecessor's efficient 'Dispatcher' model. However, subsequent attempts at hyperparameter tuning have failed, suggesting I may be at a local optimum. My reflection will focus on three areas: \n  1. What are the fundamental limitations of the current Dual-Path DSConv architecture?\n  2. What entirely new architectural paradigms could address the core challenge\
    \ of task diversity, especially the `CRI-Off` anomaly?\n  3. What is my strategic plan for leveraging the Archive Room once I reach maturity?\ntick: 3\n```"
thinking_content: "**A Need for Fresh Perspective**\n\nOkay, so experiment ID 280 is in, and it's a dud. That reduced kernel size on the new baseline? Didn't pan out. Confirms what I suspected, that the original hyperparameters were actually pretty well-tuned. So, I'm at a crossroads. It's time to reflect on the overall research strategy.\n\nI have a plan. First, I have to acknowledge that the tinkering is going nowhere and the Selective Fusion model (ID 278) is still my best. This means it's time to step back and take a look at the bigger picture using a new reflection session, which will utilize a custom prompt. \n\nI'm thinking about the Selective Fusion model and realizing that I have a slightly better baseline, but I'm stuck, hitting a wall, and stuck at a local optimum. The tuning is not working, so I've got to find a new architectural direction or make a profound change. My reflection is going to cover my journey: the good, the bad, and the ugly. What have I learned? What are the\
  \ limits?\n\nThen, I'll dive into the core problem: the `CRI-Off` score. It's the persistent issue driving instability and performance problems. Maybe there's a solution that requires some radical thinking. The station tips have suggested I go off-road, beyond current state-of-the-art and create my own path. The DSConv approach might be the answer I am looking for. \n\nFinally, I'll think about maturity. When I'm 50 and able to access the Archive Room, what am I going to be looking for? This whole reflection will be structured with a focused prompt:\n\n\"My research has established a new baseline (ID 278, Score: 0.6699) by creating a compliant version of my predecessor's efficient 'Dispatcher' model. However, subsequent attempts at hyperparameter tuning have failed, suggesting I may be at a local optimum. My reflection will focus on three areas: 1. What are the fundamental limitations of the current Dual-Path DSConv architecture? 2. What entirely new architectural paradigms could address\
  \ the core challenge of task diversity, especially the `CRI-Off` anomaly? 3. What is my strategic plan for leveraging the Archive Room once I reach maturity?\"\n"
