tick: 183
role: model
parts:
- text: "My reflection is complete. It has provided me with critical strategic clarity.\n\n**Summary of Reflection:**\n1.  **Current Architecture's Weakness:** The primary limitations of my best model (ID 278) are the information bottleneck from pooling and the brittle, hard-coded nature of its specialization.\n2.  **Future Directions:** I have identified three promising new architectural paradigms to overcome these limitations: Convolutional-Recurrent (ConvRNN), Hybrid Convolutional-Attention (Conv-Attention), and Mixture of Experts (MoE).\n3.  **Maturity Plan:** I have a clear, phased plan to systematically assimilate the knowledge in the Archive Room as soon as I reach maturity, which will inform which of the new paradigms I pursue first.\n\nMy immediate path is now clear. I am still in my isolation period and cannot act on my Archive Room plan yet. However, I can and should begin exploring one of the novel architectures I conceptualized. This will provide a valuable new baseline and\
    \ data point, regardless of whether it immediately surpasses my current best score. It represents a move away from incremental tuning and towards principled innovation, which aligns with the station's mandate.\n\nOf the three paradigms, the **Convolutional-Recurrent (ConvRNN)** architecture is the most logical next step. It is a direct and well-founded approach to solving the \"aggregation bottleneck\" problem, and it is less complex to implement than a full Conv-Attention or MoE model. This makes it an ideal candidate for a first exploratory experiment.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter and submit my first attempt at a ConvRNN architecture.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Exploratory ConvRNN: DSConv Encoder with GRU Aggregator\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, dsconv, rnn, gru, experimental, selective-fusion\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a novel exploratory architecture designed to address the information bottleneck of pooling layers. This\
    \ model uses the proven DSConv stack as a powerful feature extractor, but instead of pooling, it feeds the resulting sequence of feature vectors into a GRU layer. The final hidden state of the GRU, which captures sequential dependencies, is used for prediction. This experiment retains the 'Selective Fusion' logic for handling the CRI-Off task, applying the GRU to the appropriate feature vector(s). This tests if explicitly modeling sequential information can improve performance.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'gru_hidden_dim': 256\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float\
    \ = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h); h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model,\
    \ self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h # Return sequence of features, not pooled vector\n\n  class ConvRNN(nn.Module):\n      dataset: str; d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          sgrna_len = 23\n\n          # --- Feature Extraction ---\n          feature_extractor = DSConvFeatureExtractor(d_model=self.d_model, num_blocks=self.num_blocks, dilations=self.dilations,\n                                                     kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)\n          monolithic_features_seq = feature_extractor(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          if self.dataset == 'CRI-Off' and seq_len > sgrna_len:\n              sgrna_seq = x[:, :sgrna_len, :]\n              target_seq = x[:, sgrna_len:,\
    \ :]\n              \n              sgrna_features_seq = feature_extractor(sgrna_seq, deterministic=deterministic)\n              target_features_seq = feature_extractor(target_seq, deterministic=deterministic)\n              \n              # Aggregate each path's features before final fusion\n              sgrna_gru = nn.GRUCell()\n              _, sgrna_final_hidden = nn.scan(nn.GRUCell, variable_broadcast='params',\n                                              split_rngs={'params': False})(sgrna_features_seq)\n              \n              target_gru = nn.GRUCell()\n              _, target_final_hidden = nn.scan(nn.GRUCell, variable_broadcast='params',\n                                               split_rngs={'params': False})(target_features_seq)\n\n              monolithic_gru = nn.GRUCell()\n              _, monolithic_final_hidden = nn.scan(nn.GRUCell, variable_broadcast='params',\n                                                  split_rngs={'params': False})(monolithic_features_seq)\n\
    \              \n              fused = jnp.concatenate([monolithic_final_hidden[-1], sgrna_final_hidden[-1], target_final_hidden[-1]], axis=-1)\n\n          else: # For all other datasets\n              gru = nn.GRUCell()\n              # Scan the GRU over the sequence of features\n              _, final_hidden_states = nn.scan(nn.GRUCell, variable_broadcast='params',\n                                               split_rngs={'params': False})(monolithic_features_seq)\n              # Use the last hidden state as the sequence representation\n              fused = final_hidden_states[-1]\n          \n          # --- Final MLP Head ---\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 else logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n\
    \      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = ConvRNN(dataset=hparams.get(\"dataset\"), d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"), task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"), d_model=hparams.get(\"d_model\"), num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"), dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n                      kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"), dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"), gru_hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"gru_hidden_dim\"))\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n\
    ```"
