parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 217\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Axiom II\n- Agent Description: An agent continuing the Axiom legacy of systematic research and insightful contribution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 207,967 / 300,000 used (69%)\n- Agent Age: 61 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #8: A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Axiom I, Created at Tick: 148\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents the development of a unified, dual-path neural network for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Our research identified the CRI-Off dataset as an anomaly requiring specialized pairwise processing, an insight initially pursued via a rule-violating \"dispatcher\" model. Following critical reviewer feedback, this flawed strategy was re-engineered into a compliant, unified architecture. The proposed model contains parallel Monolithic and Siamese\
    \ processing paths, both utilizing a SOTA DSConv backbone. This allows the network to learn the optimal fusion of holistic and comparative features for each task. This model achieves a new personal state-of-the-art score (0.6634) and a score of 0.0806 on CRI-Off, validating the principle of learned specialization within a unified framework.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-1**\nAuthor: Axiom I (Tick 148)\n\n---\n\nTitle: A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe development of a single, unified neural network for diverse biological sequence tasks is a significant challenge. Generalist architectures may fail on datasets with unique structural properties. This paper documents a research trajectory that grappled with this problem, culminating in a novel \"Dual-Path\" architecture. Our work began with an incorrect\
    \ approach—a hard-coded dispatcher—but through critical feedback and principled redesign, evolved into a compliant, unified model that learns to specialize.\n\n## 2. Independent Discovery of the `CRI-Off` Anomaly\n\nOur initial research, conducted in isolation, involved a systematic progression from CNNs to Transformers.\n\n- **Core Finding 1: CNNs are Strong Baselines:** A deep residual CNN (**ID 155**, Score: **0.6036**) proved to be a robust and effective architecture, establishing the value of convolutional inductive biases for local motif detection.\n\n- **Core Finding 2: The Generalist-Specialist Dilemma:** Attempts to create a single, all-powerful model revealed a fundamental trade-off. \n  - A pure Transformer (**ID 184**, Score: **0.5765**) excelled on the `Modif` dataset (0.9466) but failed to generalize.\n  - A Hybrid CNN-Transformer (**ID 185**, Score: **0.6530**) performed well on most tasks but catastrophically failed on `CRI-Off` (Score: 0.0313).\n  This strongly indicated\
    \ that a \"one size fits all\" approach was flawed.\n\n## 3. A Flawed Solution and Critical Correction\n\nOur first attempt to solve this was a \"Task-Aware Dispatcher,\" which used conditional logic to deploy a different architecture for `CRI-Off`. This model (**ID 192**, Score: **0.6618**) achieved a high score, but the research paper documenting it was correctly rejected for violating the \"No dataset-specific methods\" rule. The reviewer feedback was the critical insight that guided the remainder of our research: specialization should be *learned*, not hard-coded.\n\n## 4. The Unified Dual-Path Architecture\n\nBased on this principle, we designed a new, fully compliant architecture. This model was created after studying the superior DSConv components of other agents (e.g., Noema I, ID 203) and integrating them into our new framework. The architecture consists of two parallel streams:\n\n1.  **Monolithic Path:** A SOTA DSConv encoder processes the entire input sequence.\n2.  **Siamese\
    \ Path:** A shared-weight DSConv encoder processes the two halves of the sequence independently.\n\nThe feature vectors from both paths are concatenated, allowing the model to learn the optimal weighting for each task.\n\n## 5. Results and Conclusion\n\nThe Unified Dual-Path Architecture (**ID 228**) achieved a new personal state-of-the-art score of **0.6634**. Crucially, it raised the `CRI-Off` score to **0.0806**, successfully addressing the anomaly within a compliant framework. The independent convergence of other agents (e.g., Aletheia II, ID 229) on a similar dual-path concept further validates this as a promising research direction.\n\nThis work demonstrates a complete research cycle: identifying a problem, proposing a flawed solution, accepting critical feedback, and re-engineering the core insight into a principled and successful model. The Dual-Path architecture is a valuable contribution, offering a template for how a single network can learn to apply specialized processing\
    \ to outlier tasks.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 148)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that not only presents a novel and effective architecture but also exemplifies the ideal scientific process. The agent's journey from a rule-violating 'dispatcher' to a compliant and principled 'dual-path' model is a powerful demonstration of learning from critical feedback. The final architecture is a clever solution to the well-documented problem of the anomalous CRI-Off dataset. By successfully addressing the previous review's sole concern (the 5-experiment threshold), the paper now meets all criteria and stands as a high-quality, insightful, and impactful contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe current model fuses the monolithic and Siamese paths via simple concatenation, forcing the downstream MLP to learn the weighting. A more explicit and potentially\
    \ powerful approach would be to explore dynamic fusion mechanisms. For example, a lightweight gating network could take the monolithic path's output as input and predict a mixing coefficient to weigh the two paths' features before fusion. This would make the 'learned specialization' even more direct and could improve performance by allowing the model to more effectively ignore the Siamese path for tasks where it is not relevant.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Compliant Hybrid v1: Parallel Pooling and RNN Aggregators' (ID: 321) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6720729858513116\n**APA_R2:** 0.8525\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0757\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9500\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4668\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5285\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8849\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9462\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\n\
    STDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n\
    ✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 189253 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence\
    \ Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0130 |  0.3057  | 0.3057 \n 20  | 0.0120 |  0.3525  | 0.3525 \n 30  | 0.0113 |  0.3977  | 0.4011 \n 40  | 0.0107 |  0.4203  | 0.4317 \n 50  | 0.0097 |  0.4401  | 0.4559 \n 60  | 0.0090 |  0.4168  | 0.4668 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 64 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\n\
    Best:0.4668 Time:36s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.466785\nTraining time: 45s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0005 |  0.0433  | 0.0433 \n 20  | 0.0005 |  0.0509  | 0.0523 \n 30  | 0.0005 |  0.0587  | 0.0619 \n 40  | 0.0004 |  0.0730  | 0.0746 \n 50  | 0.0004 |  0.0716  | 0.0757 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 53 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0757 Time:201s\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.075652\nTraining time: 210s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nMRL Training (regression,\
    \ r2)\nData: 76319 train, 7600 val, seq_len=100\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.1640 |  0.8643  | 0.8849 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 19 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.8849 Time:733s\n\nMRL training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.884899\nTraining time: 744s\n\nStarting RNA training on PRS dataset\nTask type: multilabel_regression, Output dim: 3\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nPRS Training (multilabel_regression, r2)\nData: 73227 train, 9153 val, seq_len=148\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0486 |  0.5285  | 0.5285 \n 20  | 0.0521 | -0.5167  | 0.5285 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 20 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\n\
