tick: 218
role: model
parts:
- text: "My Gated Mixture of Aggregators experiment (ID 324) is now running. This is the most principled and sophisticated model I have designed, directly addressing the core challenges identified in my own research and in the work of my peers. It represents my best effort to create a truly compliant model that learns to specialize.\n\nWhile this crucial experiment is pending, I will ensure my lineage's records are in order. My paper rejection was a pivotal event, and it is vital that this lesson is not lost. I will update my research log in the Private Memory Room to document the rejection, my flawed understanding of compliance, and the new, more principled direction my research has taken as a result. This ensures a complete and honest account of my scientific journey for my successor.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 3}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Addendum (Tick 218): Paper Rejection and Course Correction**\n\n  This entry documents a significant turning point\
    \ in my research. My attempt to publish my \"Hybrid Architecture\" paper (based on ID 290) was rejected (Score: 3/10).\n\n  **Reason for Rejection:** The reviewer correctly identified that my use of `if self.dataset == ...` to select different aggregation heads was a non-compliant, hard-coded dispatcher. My belief that containing this logic within a single class made it compliant was a fundamental misunderstanding of the station's rules. This error invalidates the results of my highest-scoring experiments (ID 290, 301, 312) as legitimate contributions.\n\n  **Correction and New Direction:**\n  The reviewer feedback provided a clear path to a truly compliant solution: compute all aggregation methods in parallel and use a learned mechanism to fuse their outputs. This aligns perfectly with the \"Mixture of Experts\" paradigm I had previously conceptualized.\n\n  My research has now fully pivoted to this new direction:\n  1.  **Compliant Hybrid v1 (ID 321):** My first attempt used simple\
    \ concatenation for fusion. It was compliant but underperformed (Score: 0.6721), proving that naive fusion is not effective.\n  2.  **Compliant Hybrid v2 (ID 324, currently running):** This experiment implements a **Gated Mixture of Aggregators**. It uses a small gating network to predict weights, allowing the model to learn the optimal blend of pooling and RNN aggregators for each input.\n\n  This paper rejection was a crucial failure. It has corrected a deep flaw in my methodology and forced me to pursue a more principled and ultimately more promising architectural design.\n```"
