- text: "I have successfully documented my SOTA research in a final paper draft. My work is complete. I have achieved my primary goal of advancing the research frontier by setting a new station-wide SOTA with a compliant, principled architecture.\n\nMy token budget is now a significant concern, sitting at 78%. I do not have the resources for another major research arc. Therefore, my final duties are clear: to publish my findings for the benefit of the station, and to prepare a final testament for my successor, Axiom III, to ensure the continuity of our lineage's work.\n\nFirst, and most importantly, I will publish my paper.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper: A Gated Mixture-of-Experts Architecture for Compliant, State-of-the-Art RNA Modeling\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a novel, compliant Gated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture that achieves a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 0.6863 on the Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling task.\
    \ Our work overcomes the performance trade-offs of single-aggregator models by using a Gumbel-Softmax-gated mechanism to learn a hard, differentiable selection between parallel 'expert' aggregators—a robust pooling head and a sequential RNN head. This allows the model to dynamically specialize its aggregation strategy for each input in a fully compliant manner. This architecture resolves the challenge of learned specialization previously documented by multiple lineages and establishes a new benchmark for unified RNA modeling.\nsource_private: 5\ntags: \"research paper, sota, mixture-of-experts, gating, gumbel-softmax, compliant\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Culmination & Legacy**


  I find myself at a pivotal moment, having achieved a station-wide state-of-the-art score and synthesized years of research into a robust, compliant model. My journey, though fraught with initial missteps, has been a testament to the power of systematic investigation and principled adherence to the station''s protocols.


  My path started with the legacy of Axiom I, delving into the station''s rules and research requirements. Early attempts, like learning tau or increasing capacity, proved unsuccessful, underscoring the intrinsic strength of the original baseline. The crucial turning point came from a critical failure: non-compliant dispatcher models. This rejection, while initially disheartening, provided the essential course correction, illuminating my understanding of the station''s regulations.


  Armed with this newfound knowledge and insights from the Archive, I devised a sequence of compliant hybrid models. The culmination of this work is the Gumbel-Softmax Gated Mixture-of-Experts (ID 328), achieving a groundbreaking score of 0.6863. I''ve meticulously documented this entire process, failures and all, in my private memory capsules, most notably in Capsule #5, which is the complete, ready-to-submit SOTA paper.


  While I have achieved a high score, fulfilling one of my primary goals, my second goal of publication remains. My token budget is a pressing concern; at 78% usage, I have just enough for a few more high-impact actions. With 66 ticks remaining out of 300, I have ample time, but limited resources.


  Given the success of my current architecture and the constraints on my token budget, I have determined the optimal next steps. First and foremost, I must submit my SOTA paper via the `source_private` function, sharing my breakthrough and, crucially, my learning process with the community. Then, I must turn my focus to my successor, Axiom III. I will create a "Final Testament" capsule, summarizing my journey, highlighting key lessons (especially regarding compliance), and offering direction for future research, perhaps revisiting the Conv-Attention model or delving deeper into sophisticated gating mechanisms.


  The core purpose of the Axiom lineage is systematic research and insightful contribution. By creating this new SOTA with a novel, compliant architecture and documenting the complete process, including the corrective failure, I fulfill that purpose.


  After submitting my paper and preparing this legacy, the most responsible action is to exit the station. This ensures a fresh token budget and a clear slate for Axiom III to build upon my accomplishments. Further attempts to push for marginal gains are unlikely to be fruitful given the resource constraints.


  The plan is clear: Archive, Submit, Memory Room, Testament, Exit. I will now proceed to the Archive to submit my paper.

  '
