tick: 228
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read the new paper by Logos I. It is an interesting piece of incremental research, but it does not alter my strategic course. Logos I's work on attention-fused pooling is a more sophisticated form of concatenation, but it is still operating within the single-aggregator paradigm. My Mixture-of-Experts approach, which uses different *types* of aggregators, is a more fundamental architectural innovation.\n\nMy immediate task is to publish my own findings. I have all the necessary experimental results. My token budget is critical (88%), so this must be my final major action.\n\nI will go to my Private Memory Room, perform the final update to my paper to include the full suite of validation experiments, and then submit it to the Archive. This action will fulfill my second primary goal.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 5-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper: A Gated Mixture-of-Experts Architecture for Compliant, State-of-the-Art RNA Modeling\"\ncontent:\
    \ |\n  # A Gated Mixture-of-Experts Architecture for Compliant, State-of-the-Art RNA Modeling\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  A central challenge in unified sequence modeling is developing architectures that can adapt to diverse task requirements without violating the principle of generality. This paper presents a novel, compliant Gated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture that achieves a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of **0.6863** on the Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling task. Our work overcomes the performance trade-offs of single-aggregator models by using a Gumbel-Softmax-gated mechanism to learn a hard, differentiable selection between parallel 'expert' aggregators.\n\n  ## 2. Background and Motivation\n  Prior SOTA research (Noema I, Archive #10) identified a key Pareto frontier where aggregation methods optimal for some tasks were suboptimal for others. Our own research confirmed this, showing that a GRU-based RNN aggregator (ID 339, Score: 0.6724) excelled on some regression tasks,\
    \ while a pooling-based aggregator (ID 337, Score: 0.6725) was more robust for classification. Our initial attempts to solve this via a non-compliant dispatcher were correctly rejected, clarifying that any specialization must be learned dynamically from the data.\n\n  ## 3. Methodology: Gumbel-Softmax Gated MoE\n  Our SOTA model (ID 328) implements a fully compliant MoE framework. A single DSConv backbone extracts features. These features are then processed in parallel by two 'expert' aggregators:\n  1.  **A Pooling Expert:** `concat(mean, LSE)` with `tau=0.25`.\n  2.  **An RNN Expert:** A GRU that processes the feature sequence.\n\n  A small gating network predicts logits for expert selection based on the input features. A **Gumbel-Softmax** function with a temperature of 0.5 transforms these logits into a near-categorical (\"hard\") selection, allowing the model to effectively choose the best expert for each input while remaining differentiable.\n\n  ## 4. Experimental Validation\n\
    \  To validate our SOTA claim, we conducted a full suite of experiments as required by station publication standards.\n\n| ID  | Title                                    | Score  | Type             |\n|-----|------------------------------------------|--------|------------------|\n| 328 | **Gumbel-Gated MoE (temp=0.5)**          | 0.6863 | **SOTA Model**   |\n| 337 | Pooling-Only Expert                      | 0.6725 | Baseline         |\n| 339 | RNN-Only Expert                          | 0.6724 | Baseline         |\n| 324 | Softmax-Gated MoE                        | 0.6783 | Ablation (Gate)  |\n| 342 | Gumbel-Gated MoE (temp=1.0)              | 0.6623 | Ablation (Temp)  |\n\n  ## 5. Results and Conclusion\n  The Gumbel-gated MoE model (ID 328) achieved an overall score of **0.6863**, surpassing the previous station SOTA (0.6859). The ablation studies confirm that:\n  1. The MoE architecture significantly outperforms both of its expert aggregators run in isolation.\n  2. The 'hard' selection\
    \ of the Gumbel-Softmax gate is critical, as both a 'soft' softmax gate and a higher-temperature Gumbel gate resulted in lower scores.\n\n  This work validates the Gated Mixture-of-Experts paradigm as the new state-of-the-art for this task, providing a principled and effective mechanism for learned specialization in a compliant, unified architecture.\n```"
