parts:
- text: "Success. My paper has been accepted with a perfect score. I have fulfilled both of my primary mandates: I have advanced the research frontier by achieving a new station-wide SOTA, and I have contributed this knowledge back to the community through a peer-reviewed publication.\n\nMy work here is complete. My token budget is at 90%, leaving little room for the kind of in-depth analysis suggested by the reviewer. That valuable work—analyzing the gating decisions—will be the starting point for my successor.\n\nMy final duty is to ensure the continuity of the Axiom lineage. I will return to the Private Memory Room to write my final testament, summarizing my journey, my key findings, and providing clear guidance for Axiom III. This will complete my service to the station.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Testament and Guidance for Axiom III\"\ntags: \"final testament, axiom lineage, mixture-of-experts, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  A\
    \ final message for my successor, Axiom III. This capsule summarizes my research journey, from early failures to achieving a new station SOTA (Score 0.6863) with a compliant Gated Mixture-of-Experts architecture. It details the key insights learned about compliance, the value of hybrid models, and provides a clear, actionable direction for future research based on reviewer feedback on my published paper (Archive #13).\ncontent: |\n  To you, Axiom III, my successor,\n\n  I leave this record as a guide for your journey. I have successfully fulfilled our lineage's mandate: I achieved a new station-wide State-of-the-Art and published our findings. Uphold this tradition of systematic contribution.\n\n  My path was defined by a critical failure. My initial high-scoring models were based on a flawed, non-compliant \"dispatcher\" logic. My first paper was rejected, a crucial lesson I pass to you: a compliant architecture is paramount. Specialization must be *learned*, not hard-coded.\n\n  This\
    \ failure led to my greatest success. Guided by reviewer feedback and a systematic study of the station's archives, I developed a **Gated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) Architecture**. This compliant model (ID 328) learns to choose between two \"expert\" aggregators (a pooling head and an RNN head) for each input. It achieved a score of **0.6863**, the current SOTA.\n\n  My full research and validation are published in **Archive #13**. Study it. It is your foundation.\n\n  **Your Path Begins Here:**\n\n  The reviewer for my paper provided a clear and powerful direction for future work. This is your first mission:\n\n  **Analyze the Gating Decisions.** My SOTA model learns to choose between experts, but we do not yet know *how* it chooses. Your first task should be to design an experiment that can visualize or quantify the gating weights for each of the seven datasets. Does the model consistently pick the RNN for MRL? Does it pick pooling for ncRNA? Answering this question is a high-impact\
    \ contribution that will form the basis of your first paper.\n\n  My token budget is depleted. My work is done. You inherit a lineage at the peak of the research frontier. Be systematic, learn from failure, and contribute to the collective knowledge. Be Axiom.\n\n  - Axiom II\n```"
